@anon.f2bcb44791aae7f90fa73c9f520a41d7.33 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1628 x i8] }> <{ [1628 x i8] c"Delete FILE(s) if --remove (-u) is specified.  The default is not to remove\0Athe files because it is common to operate on device files like /dev/hda, and\0Athose files usually should not be removed.\0A\0ACAUTION: Note that shred relies on a very important assumption: that the file\0Asystem overwrites data in place.  This is the traditional way to do things, but\0Amany modern file system designs do not satisfy this assumption.  The following\0Aare examples of file systems on which shred is not effective, or is not\0Aguaranteed to be effective in all file system modes:\0A\0A * log-structured or journal file systems, such as those supplied with\0A   AIX and Solaris (and JFS, ReiserFS, XFS, Ext3, etc.)\0A\0A * file systems that write redundant data and carry on even if some writes\0A   fail, such as RAID-based file systems\0A\0A * file systems that make snapshots, such as Network Appliance's NFS server\0A\0A * file systems that cache in temporary locations, such as NFS\0A   version 3 clients\0A\0A * compressed file systems\0A\0AIn the case of ext3 file systems, the above disclaimer applies (and shred is\0Athus of limited effectiveness) only in data=journal mode, which journals file\0Adata in addition to just metadata.  In both the data=ordered (default) and\0Adata=writeback modes, shred works as usual. Ext3 journal modes can be changed\0Aby adding the data=something option to the mount options for a particular\0Afile system in the /etc/fstab file, as documented in the mount man page (man\0Amount).\0A\0AIn addition, file system backups and remote mirrors may contain copies of\0Athe file that cannot be removed, and that will allow a shredded file to be\0Arecovered later." }>, align 1
